on $X$ if and only if $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty = 0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e46e341cd7c76f6964443483","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is boundedness relevant for the uniform norm?","options":["It ensures the supremum of $|f|$ is finite.","It makes the function constant.","It makes the domain finite.","It removes absolute values from the formula."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a76e639eec27415a202ce87f","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $\\|f\\|_\\infty$ measure?","options":["The largest size of $f$ over its whole domain, in the supremum sense","The value of $f$ at one chosen point of the domain, usually its left endpoint.","The average of $|f|$ over its whole domain, in the integral sense","The largest value of $f$ itself, without absolute values, as a supremum"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"01b352f67a013857eef1a773","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x)=2$ on $X$, what is $\\|f\\|_\\infty$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$4$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bec5930ce5e5b900d5f7c30c","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x)=x$ on $[-1,1]$, what is $\\|f\\|_\\infty$?","options":["$1$","$0$","$-1$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ace6e31932e2efa71d5e94b6","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function has uniform norm $1$ on $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["$g(x)=x$","$g(x)=2x$","$g(x)=x^2$","$g(x)=\\sin x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5b79cefba2b8ffc9f829cad","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty \\lt 0.01$, what does this say about every $x\\in X$?","options":["$|f_n(x)-f(x)| \\lt 0.01$","$|f_n(x)-f(x)| \\gt 0.01$","$f_n(x)=f(x)$ exactly","$f_n$ is unbounded"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1890f438e9f500089c0b7a81","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_009_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f,g\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be bounded functions with $\\|f\\|_\\infty=2$ and $\\|g\\|_\\infty=3$. Which statement about $\\|f+g\\|_\\infty$ must hold?","options":["$\\|f+g\\|_\\infty\\le5$","$\\|f+g\\|_\\infty=5$","$\\|f+g\\|_\\infty=3$","$\\|f+g\\|_\\infty=\\sqrt{13}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8cc7f67f1fd4d24a8a2d5656","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_011_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f_n(x)=x^n$ on $[0,1)$ and let $f$ be the zero function. Although $f_n(x)\\to0$ for every $x\\in[0,1)$, what is $\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty$, and does $f_n\\rightrightarrows f$ on $[0,1)$?","options":["$\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty=1$ for every $n$, so $f_n$ does not converge uniformly to $f$.","$\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty=1$ for every $n$, but convergence at every point still gives $f_n\\rightrightarrows f$.","$\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty\\to0$ because $x^n\\to0$ at every $x$, so $f_n\\rightrightarrows f$.","$\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty$ is undefined because the supremum of $x^n$ on $[0,1)$ is not attained."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dd93f96d4440bc2608da1431","time_limit":55},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_uniform_norm_699a0953_012_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f_n$ and $f$ be functions on a set $X$ with $\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty=\\frac{1}{n}$ for every $n$. What follows?","options":["$f_n\\rightrightarrows f$ on $X$.","$f_n\\to f$ pointwise on $X$, but not uniformly.","$f_n=f$ on $X$ for all sufficiently large $n$.","$f_n\\rightrightarrows f$ on $X$ only if $f$ is in addition bounded."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1e985ee23e39d13c6ce6698b","time_limit":40}]}}